IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `eia-core-interpretation`
IMPORT `ei4-part-vii-benefit-repayment`
IMPORT `ei4-seat`

-- Worked cases for ei4-seat.l4: at least one case for each of the 76 questions, with the facts a person in the seat would state.
-- Every expected value is written by hand from the text of the section named beside it, before the file was run; none is copied from
-- the encoding's output. Dollar figures and dates are invented for the cases. There is no scenario ledger for Canada, so no case names
-- a life event. Arguments are given in the order of the question's GIVEN lines.

§ `Review of a decision about my claim (ss 111 to 114)`

-- s 111. Amira's claim was refused. She now hands the Commission a record of employment it did not have: new facts are presented.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission rescind or amend its decision on my claim` TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- s 111. Nothing new and the Commission is satisfied of no error: no power under s 111.
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission rescind or amend its decision on my claim` FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 111. No new facts, but the Commission is satisfied the decision rested on a mistake as to a material fact.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission rescind or amend its decision on my claim` FALSE FALSE TRUE

-- s 112(1). Ben (the claimant) was told of the decision on 1 March 2026 and asks on 31 March 2026, the thirtieth day: in time.
-- Arguments: claimant, employer, prescribed form, decision day, request day, further time allowed, last day of it, under the Regulations, write-off.
#ASSERT `May I ask the Commission to reconsider its decision and is my request in time` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 31) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(1). On 2 April 2026, the thirty-second day, with no further time allowed: too late.
#ASSERT NOT `May I ask the Commission to reconsider its decision and is my request in time` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 2) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(1)(b). The Commission allowed Ben until 30 June 2026: 2 April is in time.
#ASSERT `May I ask the Commission to reconsider its decision and is my request in time` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 2) TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 6 30) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(1). Ben's employer, Chen Ltd, may ask as well.
#ASSERT `May I ask the Commission to reconsider its decision and is my request in time` FALSE TRUE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 10) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112.1. The decision is a Regulations decision about writing off an amount Ben owes: it cannot be reconsidered, however prompt he is.
#ASSERT NOT `May I ask the Commission to reconsider its decision and is my request in time` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 10) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) TRUE TRUE
-- s 112(2). The Commission's duty: Ben's prompt request must be reconsidered.
#ASSERT `Must the Commission reconsider its decision if I ask` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 31) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(2). Ben's late request, with no further time: no duty.
#ASSERT NOT `Must the Commission reconsider its decision if I ask` TRUE FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 2) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(2). A request that is not in the prescribed form and manner is not "made under subsection (1)".
#ASSERT NOT `Must the Commission reconsider its decision if I ask` TRUE FALSE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 3 10) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- s 112(1)(a). Decision communicated 1 March 2026: the thirtieth day after is 31 March 2026.
#ASSERT `What is the last day I can ask for a reconsideration without more time` (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 3 31

-- s 113. Ben, dissatisfied with the reconsideration decision, may appeal to the Employment Insurance Board of Appeal.
#ASSERT `Can I appeal to the Employment Insurance Board of Appeal` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- s 113. Ben is content with the decision: nothing to appeal.
#ASSERT NOT `Can I appeal to the Employment Insurance Board of Appeal` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- s 113. Dissatisfied with a decision refusing him more time to ask for reconsideration: appealable ("including").
#ASSERT `Can I appeal to the Employment Insurance Board of Appeal` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE

-- s 114(1). Carla's claim was allowed by the General Division on 1 May 2026; the Commission appealed on 10 May on a ground of error in law
-- (not s 36), and no regulation applies: she is paid while the appeal is pending.
-- Arguments: General Division, Board of Appeal, decision day, appeal day, section 36 ground, prescribed exception.
#ASSERT `Am I paid benefits while an appeal of the decision allowing my claim is pending` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 10) FALSE FALSE
-- s 114(2)(a). Dev's claim was allowed on 1 May 2026; the Commission appealed on 15 May, within 21 days, on the ground that Dev ought to be
-- disentitled under section 36: subsection (1) does not apply, so Dev is not paid under it.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I paid benefits while an appeal of the decision allowing my claim is pending` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 15) TRUE FALSE
-- s 114(2)(a). The same appeal brought on 23 May, the twenty-second day: the exception does not apply, so Dev is paid.
#ASSERT `Am I paid benefits while an appeal of the decision allowing my claim is pending` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 23) TRUE FALSE
-- s 114(1). Allowed by the Board of Appeal rather than the General Division.
#ASSERT `Am I paid benefits while an appeal of the decision allowing my claim is pending` FALSE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 10) FALSE FALSE
-- s 114(2)(b). A case the Regulations prescribe (regs 80 to 82) also stops it.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I paid benefits while an appeal of the decision allowing my claim is pending` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 10) FALSE TRUE
-- s 114(1). Carla was paid after the decision; the money is not recoverable from her even if the appeal goes against her.
#ASSERT `Are the benefits paid to me after the decision protected from recovery` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 10) FALSE FALSE TRUE
-- s 114(1). Benefits paid before the decision are not within the sentence.
#ASSERT NOT `Are the benefits paid to me after the decision protected from recovery` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 5 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 5 10) FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `Investigations, prosecutions and proof against me (ss 124 and 125)`

-- s 124(3). The Commission means to investigate a matter it may investigate: it must give public notice.
#ASSERT `Must the Commission give public notice before it investigates a matter` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Must the Commission give public notice before it investigates a matter` TRUE FALSE
-- s 124(3). Elena, a fishers' association officer, appears to have an interest in a matter under investigation and has made a submission.
#ASSERT `Must the Commission receive my representations on a matter it is investigating` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Must the Commission receive my representations on a matter it is investigating` TRUE FALSE TRUE
-- s 125(2). Faisal is charged in one information with two offences under the Act (not Part IV): he is barred from objecting on that ground.
#ASSERT `Am I barred from objecting that the proceeding relates to two or more offences` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I barred from objecting that the proceeding relates to two or more offences` TRUE FALSE
-- s 125(3). Faisal lives in Moncton; a provincial court judge in Moncton may hear a charge that arose elsewhere.
#ASSERT `Can a provincial court judge hear the charge against me although the matter did not arise in the judge's jurisdiction` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 125(3). He has no connection with the judge's jurisdiction.
#ASSERT NOT `Can a provincial court judge hear the charge against me although the matter did not arise in the judge's jurisdiction` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 125(4). Gina: the Commission became aware on 15 March 2026; a prosecution commenced on 15 March 2031 is in time, on 16 March 2031 is not.
#ASSERT `Can a prosecution against me still be commenced` FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 15) (YearMonthDay 2031 3 15) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Can a prosecution against me still be commenced` FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 15) (YearMonthDay 2031 3 16) EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- s 125(4). The offence is under Part IV: the section does not speak. NOT COVERED: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `Can a prosecution against me still be commenced` TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 15) (YearMonthDay 2031 3 16) EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `What is the last day a prosecution against me can be commenced` FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 15) EQUALS JUST (YearMonthDay 2031 3 15)
#ASSERT `What is the last day a prosecution against me can be commenced` TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 15) EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 125(5). A certificate that appears to come from the Commission and certifies the day it became aware: conclusive proof.
#ASSERT `Is a certificate from the Commission of the day it became aware conclusive proof against me` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is a certificate from the Commission of the day it became aware conclusive proof against me` FALSE TRUE
-- s 125(6). Hugo: an affidavit sworn before a commissioner of oaths by a person acting for the Commission, saying it has charge of the
-- records, that the demand was served on Hugo personally on a named day, and attaching a true copy.
-- Arguments: sworn, before a commissioner, before another authorized person, acts for the Commission, personal service provided for,
-- states charge, states service, identifies exhibit.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit that a request, notice or demand was served on me personally` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s 125(6). The same affidavit not sworn or affirmed before anyone: not an affidavit.
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit that a request, notice or demand was served on me personally` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s 125(7). Hugo did not file a statement; an affidavit says no record of it was found after a careful search.
-- Arguments: sworn, before a commissioner, before another authorized person, acts for the Commission, required to file, states charge, states search.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit that I did not file a return, statement, answer or certificate` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit that I did not file a return, statement, answer or certificate` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- s 125(8). An affidavit sworn before another authorized person says the records show Hugo's return was filed on a particular day.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit the day I filed a return, statement, answer or certificate` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission prove by affidavit the day I filed a return, statement, answer or certificate` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s 125(14). A return in Hugo's name is produced in his prosecution; it appears to have been filed by him and there is no contrary evidence.
#ASSERT `Does the production of a return in court prove that I filed or signed it` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- s 125(14). Hugo produces evidence that he did not file it: the proof is displaced.
#ASSERT NOT `Does the production of a return in court prove that I filed or signed it` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
-- s 125(17). Ines is a director of a corporation that commits an offence under the Act; she authorized it: she is guilty of it too.
#ASSERT `Am I guilty of my corporation's offence as an officer, director or agent` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 125(17). A director who did none of the five things is not caught.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I guilty of my corporation's offence as an officer, director or agent` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE

§ `Money I owe the Commission: certificates, judgments and my pay (s 126(1) to (6))`

-- s 126(1). Jamal owes an overpayment under Part I, unpaid since 1 January 2026, and the Commission does not think he is avoiding payment.
-- On 31 January the 30 days since the default have not expired; on 1 February they have.
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission certify an amount I owe and have not paid` `Part I` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 1 31)
#ASSERT `Can the Commission certify an amount I owe and have not paid` `Part I` TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 2 1)
-- s 126(1)(a). If the Commission thinks Jamal is attempting to avoid payment it may certify without delay.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission certify an amount I owe and have not paid` `Part I` TRUE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 1 2)
#ASSERT `From what day can the Commission certify my unpaid amount if it does not think I am avoiding payment` (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 2 1
-- s 126(2). The certificate is registered; the amount specified is 1,000 and interest to the day of payment 25: a judgment for 1,025.
#ASSERT `How much is the Federal Court judgment against me once the Commission registers its certificate` TRUE 1000 25 EQUALS JUST 1025
#ASSERT `How much is the Federal Court judgment against me once the Commission registers its certificate` FALSE 1000 25 EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 126(3). Reasonable costs attendant on registration are recoverable.
#ASSERT `Can the costs of registering the certificate be recovered from me` TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(4). Jamal's employer owes him wages; the Commission knows it, Jamal is liable under Part I, and the notice goes by registered mail.
-- Arguments: knows or suspects, liable under Part I, II or VII.1, liable under 126(7), served personally, sent by certified/registered mail or similar.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission make someone who owes me money pay it to the Receiver General` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can the Commission make someone who owes me money pay it to the Receiver General` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 126(5)(a). The requirement already made on Jamal's employer covers his later pay until he has satisfied what he owes.
#ASSERT `Does the Commission's requirement cover my future pay from my employer` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Does the Commission's requirement cover my future pay from my employer` TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(6). The employer paid 200 to the Receiver General and holds the Commission's receipt: 200 of what it owed Jamal is discharged;
-- of 500 owed, Jamal can still claim 300.
#ASSERT `How much of what my employer owed me is discharged by its payment to the Receiver General` TRUE 200 EQUALS JUST 200
#ASSERT `How much of what my employer owed me can I still claim from it` TRUE 200 500 EQUALS JUST 300

§ `Inspections and demands for documents (s 126(10) to (15), (21))`

-- s 126(10). Kaito runs a small fishing business. An authorized person asks, at a reasonable time and for the Act's purposes, to inspect
-- his books of account.
#ASSERT `Can an authorized person inspect or examine my documents` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Can an authorized person inspect or examine my documents` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
-- s 126(10)(a). The authorized person believes on reasonable grounds that Kaito's records are kept at his shop (not a dwelling-house).
-- Arguments: authorized, purpose, believes employed, believes records, residence, yard building, mobile unit, consent, warrant.
#ASSERT `Can an authorized person enter my premises or my home without my consent` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 126(11). The records are in his house: without consent or a warrant, no entry.
#ASSERT NOT `Can an authorized person enter my premises or my home without my consent` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 126(11). With a warrant the entry is permitted.
#ASSERT `Can an authorized person enter my premises or my home without my consent` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
-- s 2(1) "dwelling-house": a mobile unit being used as a residence is one, so without consent or a warrant no entry.
#ASSERT NOT `Can an authorized person enter my premises or my home without my consent` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- s 126(10)(b). Kaito owns the shop: he must give reasonable assistance and answer proper questions.
#ASSERT `Must I give an authorized person reasonable assistance and answer proper questions at my premises` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Must I give an authorized person reasonable assistance and answer proper questions at my premises` FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(12). A judge, on the Commission's ex parte application, satisfied on all three points: may issue a warrant.
#ASSERT `Can a judge issue a warrant to enter my home` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can a judge issue a warrant to enter my home` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
-- s 126(13). The judge is not satisfied that entry is necessary but access was refused and the documents are in the house: may order access.
#ASSERT `Can a judge order me to give access to documents in my home instead` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Can a judge order me to give access to documents in my home instead` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(14). Kaito is served personally with a notice for a document, stating a reasonable time, not barred by (15).
-- Arguments: purpose, served personally, sent by mail or similar, reasonable time, information, document, barred by (15).
#ASSERT `Must I provide the information or document the Commission requires of me` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- s 126(14), subject to (15). The same requirement barred by subsection (15): no.
#ASSERT NOT `Must I provide the information or document the Commission requires of me` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(15). The Commission asks a bank for information about unnamed persons, without a judge's authorization: barred.
#ASSERT `Is the Commission barred from requiring a third party to give information about unnamed persons` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the Commission barred from requiring a third party to give information about unnamed persons` TRUE TRUE TRUE
-- s 126(21). Kaito blocks an authorized person at the door: he hinders what section 126 authorizes.
#ASSERT `Have I contravened the duty of compliance in section 126` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 126(21). He fails to hand over a record he cannot find: failing when unable is not a contravention.
#ASSERT NOT `Have I contravened the duty of compliance in section 126` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `Have I contravened the duty of compliance in section 126` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE

§ `What I tell the Commission, and questions that arise in court (ss 129 to 134)`

-- s 129. Lena tells the Commission, in writing, what it needs to decide a claimant's entitlement: an occasion of qualified privilege.
#ASSERT `Is what I tell the Commission protected by qualified privilege` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is what I tell the Commission protected by qualified privilege` FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- s 130. Her employer failed to issue a record of employment and, because of that, Lena lost the right to claim: the Commission may pay.
#ASSERT `May the Commission pay me although someone else's failure cost me the right to claim` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `May the Commission pay me although someone else's failure cost me the right to claim` TRUE FALSE
-- s 131(1). A section 90 question about Lena's insurable employment arises in a lawsuit and has not been decided by the officer: refer and wait.
#ASSERT `Must the court send a question about my insurable employment to the Canada Revenue Agency and wait` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Must the court send a question about my insurable employment to the Canada Revenue Agency and wait` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `Must the court go on with the hearing once the Canada Revenue Agency's ruling is received` TRUE TRUE
-- s 131(2). Appeals under s 91 and s 103 are pending: the court defers judgment until each decision is received.
#ASSERT `Must the court wait for the Minister of National Revenue's decision on my appeal before giving judgment` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the court wait for the Tax Court of Canada's decision on my appeal before giving judgment` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Must the court wait for the Tax Court of Canada's decision on my appeal before giving judgment` TRUE TRUE
-- s 132. A question the Commission could decide arises in a suit; an appeal from a Commission decision is pending; the decision is conclusive.
#ASSERT `Must the court send a question the Commission could decide to the Commission and wait` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the court wait for the appeal decision if an appeal from the Commission's decision is pending` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the court go on with the hearing once it receives the decision` TRUE
#ASSERT `Is the decision conclusive in proceedings under the Act` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Is the decision conclusive in proceedings under the Act` TRUE TRUE
-- s 134(2). A certificate of the Commission says a notice was mailed to Lena: evidence she received it in the ordinary course of the mails.
#ASSERT `Is a certificate that a notice was mailed to me evidence that I received it` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is a certificate that a notice was mailed to me evidence that I received it` TRUE FALSE

§ `Offences and punishment (ss 135 to 137)`

-- s 135(1). Mateo, claiming benefits, made a representation he knew was false: guilty under paragraph (a).
#ASSERT `Am I guilty of an offence under section 135(1)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 135(1). Nadia did none of the listed things.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I guilty of an offence under section 135(1)` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 135(1)(b.1). Mateo also knowingly failed to declare earnings.
#ASSERT `Am I guilty of an offence under section 135(1)` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
-- s 135(2). A penalty for the offence was already imposed under section 38: no prosecution may be instituted.
#ASSERT NOT `Can I still be prosecuted for the offence if I was already given a penalty` TRUE
#ASSERT `Can I still be prosecuted for the offence if I was already given a penalty` FALSE
-- s 135(3)(a). Mateo's offence is an undeclared-earnings case: benefits reduced 1,000, would have been paid 3,000: 5,000 + 2 x 4,000 = 13,000.
-- Arguments: (b.1) case, appropriate case, reduced under 19(3), would have been paid, paid as a result.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be fined for an offence under section 135` TRUE FALSE 1000 3000 0 EQUALS 13000
-- s 135(3)(a)(ii). A false statement case the court thinks appropriate, with 2,500 paid as a result: 5,000 + 2 x 2,500 = 10,000.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be fined for an offence under section 135` FALSE TRUE 0 0 2500 EQUALS 10000
-- s 135(3)(a). A case with no addition: 5,000.
#ASSERT `What is the most I can be fined for an offence under section 135` FALSE FALSE 0 0 0 EQUALS 5000
-- s 135(3). A fine of 13,000 and six months in the (b.1) case is within the limits; 13,001 is not.
-- Arguments: (b.1) case, appropriate case, reduced, would have paid, paid as a result, the fine, months.
#ASSERT `Is this fine and prison term within what section 135(3) allows` TRUE FALSE 1000 3000 0 13000 6
#ASSERT NOT `Is this fine and prison term within what section 135(3) allows` TRUE FALSE 1000 3000 0 13001 6
-- s 136(1). Olga contravenes the Act: guilty of an offence.
#ASSERT `Am I guilty of an offence for contravening the Act or the regulations` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I guilty of an offence for contravening the Act or the regulations` FALSE
-- s 136(2). Pedro obstructs an authorized person who is performing their duties.
#ASSERT `Am I guilty of an offence for delaying or obstructing a person authorized by the Commission` FALSE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I guilty of an offence for delaying or obstructing a person authorized by the Commission` FALSE TRUE FALSE
-- s 137. Olga's offence has no penalty of its own: the general penalty applies.
#ASSERT `Am I liable to the general penalty for my offence` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Am I liable to the general penalty for my offence` TRUE TRUE
-- s 137. A fine of 100 is the least; 99 is below it; 2,000 is the most.
-- Arguments: a fine is imposed, the fine, imprisonment imposed, months.
#ASSERT `Is this punishment within the general penalty` TRUE 100 FALSE 0
#ASSERT NOT `Is this punishment within the general penalty` TRUE 99 FALSE 0
#ASSERT `Is this punishment within the general penalty` TRUE 2000 FALSE 0

§ `My Social Insurance Number (ss 138 and 139)`

-- s 138. Quentin is employed in insurable employment: he must have a Social Insurance Number.
#ASSERT `Must I have a Social Insurance Number` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `Must I have a Social Insurance Number` FALSE FALSE
-- s 139. Rosa has a number, married on 1 March 2026 and changed her name, and told no other authority: she must tell the Commission by 30 April 2026.
#ASSERT `Must I tell the Commission my new name after a change of name` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `By what day must I tell the Commission my new name` TRUE TRUE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) EQUALS JUST (YearMonthDay 2026 4 30)
-- s 139. Rosa already told the registry that records the change: the duty does not arise. NOT COVERED by a date: NOTHING.
#ASSERT NOT `Must I tell the Commission my new name after a change of name` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `By what day must I tell the Commission my new name` TRUE TRUE TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 3 1) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Repaying benefits because my income was high (Part VII, ss 144 to 151)`

-- s 144. Sam's income under the Income Tax Act is 80,000; he deducted 3,000 under the listed paragraphs, and 1,000 and 500 were included
-- under section 79 and paragraph 56(1)(q.1): 80,000 + 3,000 - 1,000 - 500 = 81,500.
#ASSERT `What is my income for the benefit repayment` 80000 3000 1000 500 EQUALS 81500
-- s 145(1). Tara was paid 20,000 of regular Part I benefits and 8,000 of maternity benefits in the year; her income is 100,000 and the
-- maximum yearly insurable earnings 60,000 (so 1.25 times is 75,000 and the excess 25,000). Maternity benefits are left out: 30% of the
-- lesser of 20,000 and 25,000 is 6,000. She was paid 12 weeks of regular benefits in the ten years, none before 30 June 1996.
-- Arguments: regular Part I, regular Part VIII, special Part I, Part VII.1, income, maximum, weeks in ten years, of which before 1996.
#ASSERT `How much must I repay of the benefits I was paid in the year` 20000 0 8000 0 100000 60000 12 0 EQUALS 6000
-- s 145(1). Tara's income equals 75,000: it does not exceed 1.25 times the maximum, so nothing is owed.
#ASSERT `How much must I repay of the benefits I was paid in the year` 20000 0 8000 0 75000 60000 12 0 EQUALS 0
-- s 145(2). Uma was paid no regular benefits in the ten years before the year: she repays nothing, however high her income.
#ASSERT `How much must I repay of the benefits I was paid in the year` 20000 0 0 0 100000 60000 0 0 EQUALS 0
-- s 145(1)(a) with Part VIII. Vik, a fisher, was paid 3,000 of Part VIII regular benefits and 20,000 of Part I; Part VII.1 benefits of 5,000 are
-- left out. The benefits counted are 23,000; the excess 25,000: 30% of 23,000 is 6,900.
#ASSERT `How much must I repay of the benefits I was paid in the year` 20000 3000 0 5000 100000 60000 12 0 EQUALS 6900
-- s 145(2) and (3). Twelve weeks paid, five of them beginning before 30 June 1996: seven count, so the exception does not apply.
#ASSERT NOT `Am I excused from the repayment because I was paid regular benefits for less than one week in the ten years before` 12 5
-- s 145(2) and (3). Five weeks paid, all before 30 June 1996: none counts, so Wen is excused.
#ASSERT `Am I excused from the repayment because I was paid regular benefits for less than one week in the ten years before` 5 5
-- s 145(4). Xavier is alive: the repayment for 2025 is due on or before 30 April 2026.
#ASSERT `By what day must I make the repayment` 2025 FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 4 30
-- s 145(4)(a). Yara died on 15 December 2025: her estate has six months, to 15 June 2026.
#ASSERT `By what day must I make the repayment` 2025 TRUE (YearMonthDay 2025 12 15) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 6 15
-- s 145(5). A repayment under section 145 does not reduce the weeks counted; for any other repayment the section says nothing.
#ASSERT `Does a repayment I make reduce the weeks of regular benefits counted for the ten-year exception` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Does a repayment I make reduce the weeks of regular benefits counted for the ten-year exception` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 146. Tara owes 6,000: she must file a return. With nothing owed, no return.
#ASSERT `Must I file a benefit repayment return` 6000
#ASSERT NOT `Must I file a benefit repayment return` 0
-- s 146(b). Xavier's filing-due date is 30 April 2026 and no notice has been given: that is the last day.
-- Arguments: year, died, day of death, filing-due date, notice given, notice day.
#ASSERT `By what day must the benefit repayment return be filed` 2025 FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 30) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 4 30
-- s 146(a). Yara died on 15 December 2025: her legal representative has until 15 June 2026.
#ASSERT `By what day must the benefit repayment return be filed` 2025 TRUE (YearMonthDay 2025 12 15) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 30) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 6 15
-- s 146(c). The Minister's written notice names 1 September 2026.
#ASSERT `By what day must the benefit repayment return be filed` 2025 FALSE (YearMonthDay 2026 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2026 4 30) TRUE (YearMonthDay 2026 9 1) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2026 9 1
-- s 146. Who files: Xavier files for himself; Yara's legal representative files for her; the guardian files for a claimant who cannot.
#ASSERT `Who must file the benefit repayment return` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 146(b) the claimant files the return`
#ASSERT `Who must file the benefit repayment return` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 146(a) the legal representative of the deceased claimant files the return`
#ASSERT `Who must file the benefit repayment return` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `s 146(b) the guardian, curator, tutor, committee or other legal representative of a claimant who is unable to file files the return`
-- s 147. Whoever must file under section 146 must estimate the repayment in the return.
#ASSERT `Must my return include my estimate of the benefit repayment` TRUE
-- s 148. The Minister of National Revenue administers and enforces Part VII.
#ASSERT `Who administers and enforces the benefit repayment` EQUALS `the Minister of National Revenue`
-- s 150. Tara's unpaid benefit repayment is a debt due to Her Majesty, recoverable in the Federal Court, another court, or as the Income Tax Act provides.
#ASSERT `Is what I owe under Part VII a debt due to the Crown` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Where can the Crown recover what I owe under Part VII` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST (LIST `s 150 in the Federal Court`, `s 150 in any other court of competent jurisdiction`, `s 150 in any other manner provided by the Income Tax Act`)
-- s 150. An amount that is neither: the section says nothing about recovery. NOT COVERED: NOTHING.
#ASSERT `Where can the Crown recover what I owe under Part VII` FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- s 151(1). The tax authority may give the Commission information obtained under the Income Tax Act that is needed for Part VII.
#ASSERT `May the tax authority give my tax information to the Commission` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `May the tax authority give my tax information to the Commission` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE
-- s 151(2). A person the Commission authorizes is bound by the Income Tax Act's confidentiality rules for information obtained under (1).
#ASSERT `Is a person the Commission authorizes bound by the Income Tax Act's confidentiality rules for my information` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Is a person the Commission authorizes bound by the Income Tax Act's confidentiality rules for my information` FALSE TRUE

§ `The alternate-access scheme and interim orders (Parts VIII.1 and VIII.3)`

-- s 153.1(1). Zoe is an insured person with exactly 600 hours, who does not qualify under section 7 and is not subject to a section 7.1 increase.
#ASSERT `Is the alternate-access scheme meant to give me special benefits` TRUE 600 FALSE FALSE
-- s 153.1(1). With 599 hours she is outside the class.
#ASSERT NOT `Is the alternate-access scheme meant to give me special benefits` TRUE 599 FALSE FALSE
-- s 153.1(3). Subject to a section 7.1 increase: the scheme may not give her special benefits.
#ASSERT NOT `Is the alternate-access scheme meant to give me special benefits` TRUE 600 FALSE TRUE
-- s 153.3. An interim order made on 30 September 2020 to mitigate COVID-19, adding provisions, not in respect of Part IV or VII, with the
-- Minister of Finance's consent, not in respect of Part III: validly made.
-- Arguments: COVID-19 purpose, adds/adapts/disapplies, Part IV or VII, regulation for IV or VII, Finance consent, Part III, Treasury Board consent, day.
#ASSERT `Was an interim order that changed my rights validly made` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2020 9 30)
-- s 153.3(9). Made on 1 October 2020: too late.
#ASSERT NOT `Was an interim order that changed my rights validly made` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2020 10 1)
-- s 153.3(2). An order in respect of Part VII (the benefit repayment) can never be made: benefit repayment is untouched by an interim order.
#ASSERT NOT `Was an interim order that changed my rights validly made` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE (YearMonthDay 2020 6 1)
-- s 153.3(6). An interim order may have retroactive effect if it says so.
#ASSERT `Can an interim order apply to me retroactively` TRUE
-- s 153.3(7). An order took effect on Monday 15 March 2021 with no specified day and no repeal: it ends on the Saturday after the first
-- anniversary (15 March 2022, a Tuesday), 19 March 2022.
-- Arguments: specifies a day, that day, repealed, repeal day, day it takes effect.
#ASSERT `On what day does an interim order's change stop applying to me` FALSE (YearMonthDay 2021 1 1) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2021 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2021 3 15) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2022 3 19
-- s 153.3(7)(a). The order specifies 30 June 2021: that is earlier, so it governs.
#ASSERT `On what day does an interim order's change stop applying to me` TRUE (YearMonthDay 2021 6 30) FALSE (YearMonthDay 2021 1 1) (YearMonthDay 2021 3 15) EQUALS YearMonthDay 2021 6 30
-- s 153.3(8). An order that applies despite the Act prevails where they conflict.
#ASSERT `Does an interim order prevail over the Act where they conflict` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `Does an interim order prevail over the Act where they conflict` FALSE TRUE
